1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,000
In a timeless place where nature rules,

2
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,760
ancient knowledge meets modern science,

3
00:00:12,761 --> 00:00:17,640
and cutting edge takes on new meaning.

4
00:00:17,641 --> 00:00:19,680
I've been bit 90 times.

5
00:00:19,681 --> 00:00:24,760
It's a struggle for balance between man and the swamp.

6
00:00:24,761 --> 00:00:28,240
It takes more than luck to survive here.

7
00:00:28,241 --> 00:00:32,940
It takes Swamp Tech, now on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:49,960
About 6% of Earth's dry land is actually

9
00:00:49,961 --> 00:00:54,140
a waterlogged wilderness, inaccessible and unforgiving.

10
00:00:54,141 --> 00:00:58,140
These are our wetlands, swamps, and marshes.

11
00:00:58,141 --> 00:01:04,140
More than 40% of the world's species live here, including

12
00:01:04,141 --> 00:01:07,140
man.

13
00:01:07,141 --> 00:01:10,140
In the United States, the biggest swamp lies deep in the heart

14
00:01:10,141 --> 00:01:11,140
of Louisiana.

15
00:01:11,141 --> 00:01:16,140
It's 150 miles long, and it's called the Atchafalaya.

16
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:23,140
Here, the way anywhere is tangled and plant choked.

17
00:01:23,141 --> 00:01:24,380
choked.

18
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:27,880
And just getting around is a huge challenge,

19
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:31,380
unless you have one of these, an airboat.

20
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:37,680
With an airboat, you can run marsh areas.

21
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:40,260
So many places you can go that you can't go with anything

22
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:41,220
else.

23
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:43,360
You could walk it, which would be quite miserable and take

24
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:44,660
forever.

25
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:47,160
But with an airboat, you can run the canals as fast as you'd

26
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:47,960
like.

27
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:53,140
Get out there, run the marsh, cross the land.

28
00:01:53,141 --> 00:01:54,940
There's just nothing like it.

29
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:56,440
You can't beat it.

30
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:01,880
Airboats are an important part of life in the swamp,

31
00:02:01,980 --> 00:02:03,980
and Mark Robichaux and his son Brent

32
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:06,080
have been building them for years.

33
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:12,400
An airboat is basically a flat-bottomed boat

34
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:16,000
propelled by a giant rear-mounted fan.

35
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:19,900
When the propellers spin, they produce a rearward thrust

36
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:21,940
that pushes the boat forward.

37
00:02:21,941 --> 00:02:27,280
forward, rudders behind the fan manipulate air for steering.

38
00:02:27,281 --> 00:02:29,620
Because both navigation and propulsion functions

39
00:02:29,621 --> 00:02:32,500
are in the air, the bottom of the boat

40
00:02:32,501 --> 00:02:36,760
remains entirely flat, allowing it to easily navigate

41
00:02:36,761 --> 00:02:39,860
shallow and plant-choked waters.

42
00:02:39,861 --> 00:02:43,360
In other words, it's the perfect swamp vessel,

43
00:02:43,361 --> 00:02:45,300
a simple yet highly efficient solution

44
00:02:45,301 --> 00:02:46,380
for quickly getting around.

45
00:02:46,381 --> 00:02:54,960
The Robichaux shop, like most airboat companies, is a small business, and every airboat is

46
00:02:54,961 --> 00:02:55,960
handcrafted.

47
00:02:55,961 --> 00:03:00,840
Start to finish, within three to four weeks, three and a half weeks, we could have one

48
00:03:00,841 --> 00:03:03,620
out of the door and running.

49
00:03:03,621 --> 00:03:06,500
Mark's team builds up to 45 boats each year.

50
00:03:06,501 --> 00:03:10,200
The process always begins with a hull.

51
00:03:10,201 --> 00:03:15,820
It's fabricated from sheets of marine-grade aluminum, specially formulated to resist corrosion.

52
00:03:15,821 --> 00:03:22,820
Using a press, the crew slowly crimps and bends the metal sheets into shape.

53
00:03:22,821 --> 00:03:28,820
Once the runners are welded into place, the hull is pieced together.

54
00:03:28,821 --> 00:03:33,820
This process can take up to nine days to complete.

55
00:03:33,821 --> 00:03:37,820
Finally, the crew attaches a sheet of polymer to the underside of the hull.

56
00:03:37,821 --> 00:03:44,820
This protects the aluminum and gives the boat its characteristic skimming ability.

57
00:03:44,821 --> 00:03:47,660
Aluminum doesn't slide well on grass or mud,

58
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,320
but the polymer coating on the bottom,

59
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:51,060
the sheeting that's applied there,

60
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:53,620
allows it to slide across grass, across mud,

61
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:56,960
and offer some protection on concrete and blacktop.

62
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:03,880
Meanwhile, at another station,

63
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:06,880
work is underway on the boat's key safety feature,

64
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:08,680
the prop guard.

65
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:11,920
This steel cage will enclose the boat's propellers,

66
00:04:11,921 --> 00:04:15,560
which can spin up to 2,800 revolutions per minute.

67
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:20,700
Years ago, they actually didn't have a full pop bar.

68
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:23,040
The old airboats, like my father built them,

69
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:25,420
first started years ago, very dangerous.

70
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:27,680
And I guess, you know, after a few people got cut off,

71
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:29,520
you know, hands, heads cut off,

72
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:31,500
they switched to full cages like this.

73
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:36,400
Next, the crew installs the propellers.

74
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:38,400
They're similar to airplane propellers.

75
00:04:38,401 --> 00:04:46,160
The blades are a mix of carbon fiber, Kevlar and fiberglass, and they're hollow in the

76
00:04:46,161 --> 00:04:52,340
middle, reducing their weight to just over six pounds per blade.

77
00:04:52,341 --> 00:04:56,600
The rudders are simply sheets of aluminum positioned behind the propeller system.

78
00:04:56,601 --> 00:05:02,180
Because you don't want anything below the water level to avoid tearing and hanging it

79
00:05:02,181 --> 00:05:06,180
up on things, you have to manipulate the air as opposed to the water, and the rudders do

80
00:05:06,181 --> 00:05:07,180
that.

81
00:05:07,181 --> 00:05:10,480
assembled, the real fun begins.

82
00:05:10,580 --> 00:05:13,320
Each new boat gets taken for a spin.

83
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:16,920
It's definitely the best part of the job.

84
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:19,920
We do test running at our shop, but the final test running

85
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:22,020
is putting it in the water, and I'll run it in the water,

86
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:24,160
run it on the ground, and just test everything,

87
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:26,540
steering, accelerate it, make sure everything's working,

88
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:27,740
just like we like it.

89
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:36,540
Most airboats run at an average top speed

90
00:05:36,541 --> 00:05:39,540
speed of 50 miles an hour, skimming lightly over the

91
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:40,540
surface of the water.

92
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:46,540
The boats have a steering stick that moves the rudders.

93
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:49,980
The driver pushes the stick forward to turn right and pulls

94
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:52,580
it back to go left.

95
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:54,700
Ear protection is a good idea.

96
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:58,500
Air boats are loud, thanks to engines that average about 400

97
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:00,500
horsepower.

98
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:03,540
It's an exhilarating ride, but operators shouldn't get too

99
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:05,040
carried away.

100
00:06:05,041 --> 00:06:07,980
Airboats can't go in reverse.

101
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,980
Since most boats use reverse as their braking system,

102
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:15,080
the lack of reverse also means no brakes.

103
00:06:15,180 --> 00:06:18,780
Stopping an airboat takes practice and a lot of distance.

104
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:24,300
It's got to give you an idea of where an airboat can go

105
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:26,900
and why there's a need for it.

106
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,200
Without one, you couldn't come out of these areas.

107
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:31,000
An airboat will cross all of this,

108
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:32,900
and this is what they're built for.

109
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:40,040
An airboat's versatility allows it to go almost anywhere.

110
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:51,060
The earliest airboats were used in Arctic climates

111
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:53,360
for skimming over half-frozen waters,

112
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:56,400
but the biggest and most powerful airboats in the world

113
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:58,600
are found in the swamp, and they're

114
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:01,960
built for business rather than pleasure.

115
00:07:01,961 --> 00:07:06,960
A boat like this, this is a 40-foot boat, 15 feet wide.

116
00:07:07,060 --> 00:07:08,660
There's four engines on it.

117
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:11,300
It's used for just about any type of electrical work,

118
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:13,800
oil field work, anywhere in the marshes

119
00:07:13,900 --> 00:07:17,500
or even shallow water areas where a boat is needed

120
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:21,320
that can only get through with air propulsion.

121
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:22,920
Mark specializes in building

122
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:25,160
these huge commercial airboats.

123
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:27,220
One of his most impressive is hard at work

124
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:29,620
on a construction site.

125
00:07:29,621 --> 00:07:32,420
The water here is only five feet deep,

126
00:07:32,421 --> 00:07:34,460
making this job perfect for an airboat.

127
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:38,860
Our airboat's not gonna mess up your marshland.

128
00:07:38,861 --> 00:07:40,340
You can get in real shallow water.

129
00:07:40,341 --> 00:07:41,940
We can work in deep water.

130
00:07:41,941 --> 00:07:44,260
Shallow water, you're talking a couple feet.

131
00:07:44,261 --> 00:07:46,180
Deep water, you're talking 15 feet.

132
00:07:46,181 --> 00:07:50,500
And our crane can work in either or, or on land.

133
00:07:50,501 --> 00:07:53,220
That boat has an 80-foot knuckle-boom crane on it,

134
00:07:53,221 --> 00:07:56,480
which gives it a reach that no other airboat around has.

135
00:07:56,481 --> 00:08:01,480
The boat has poles, called spuds, that act as table legs,

136
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:03,820
keeping the boat stable and in place.

137
00:08:03,920 --> 00:08:06,920
The spuds provide extra balance while the boom is raised

138
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:08,220
and lowered.

139
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:10,800
But maximum balance is in the design.

140
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:14,140
Everything on the boat is as symmetrical as possible,

141
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:16,600
and the size of the platform ensures enough water

142
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:18,400
displacement to keep things steady.

143
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:23,000
It's very impressive.

144
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:25,540
Right now, they're just sitting in the water, which actually

145
00:08:25,541 --> 00:08:28,480
which actually in the water, a boat is a little more tipsy,

146
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:33,020
so it's less stable going that far out, 80 feet out,

147
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:34,340
but it's working just fine.

148
00:08:35,660 --> 00:08:40,080
Moving a boat this big requires four 600-horsepower engines.

149
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:42,920
Each engine powers its own huge propeller.

150
00:08:43,620 --> 00:08:47,140
Between them, they put out about 8,000 pounds of thrust,

151
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:51,360
enough to keep this 15-ton boat gliding across the water.

152
00:08:51,361 --> 00:08:56,360
building big is a key component of Swamp Tech,

153
00:08:56,361 --> 00:08:58,360
and airboats are just the beginning.

154
00:08:58,361 --> 00:09:03,360
More than 1,000 miles east, deep in the swamps of South Florida,

155
00:09:03,361 --> 00:09:06,360
big comes on four wheels.

156
00:09:06,361 --> 00:09:09,360
This is a swamp buggy, the ultimate off-road vehicle,

157
00:09:09,361 --> 00:09:12,360
custom-built to withstand the roughest terrain.

158
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:18,360
Chris Tolson, also known as Bones, spent about a year building

159
00:09:18,361 --> 00:09:20,860
He spent about a year building this one,

160
00:09:20,861 --> 00:09:22,860
and he uses it for pleasure,

161
00:09:22,861 --> 00:09:24,860
taking friends and family for some bumpy rides

162
00:09:24,861 --> 00:09:26,360
through the swamp.

163
00:09:26,361 --> 00:09:29,660
It's a typical West Palm Beach style buggy,

164
00:09:29,661 --> 00:09:31,660
which means the upright and the frame

165
00:09:31,661 --> 00:09:33,660
is all built custom.

166
00:09:39,660 --> 00:09:41,860
You can build them from truck chassis,

167
00:09:41,861 --> 00:09:43,860
but in West Palm Beach, we like to build them

168
00:09:43,861 --> 00:09:46,160
with a tube chassis and then, you know,

169
00:09:46,161 --> 00:09:48,560
all the tube metal uprights and platform.

170
00:09:48,660 --> 00:09:53,060
It's a pretty traditional way we do it here.

171
00:09:53,160 --> 00:09:56,100
The chassis serves as the buggy's base,

172
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:57,600
and parts are added as needed.

173
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:05,020
Most of the parts on this buggy came from something else.

174
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:15,160
The four-foot tires are from agricultural equipment.

175
00:10:15,161 --> 00:10:20,920
The engine is from a stock car, and the running gear is from a military truck.

176
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:27,980
It's a two-and-a-half-ton Rockwell that we get from the military trucks that they're scrapping.

177
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:31,860
We'll actually get them and cut the running gear out of them and use them in our swamp buggies.

178
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:34,940
Buggies are an original Florida creation.

179
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:39,680
They were born here in the 1930s, built for hunting deep in the marsh.

180
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:43,620
But the lure of bragging rights spurred a quick evolution.

181
00:10:43,621 --> 00:10:46,800
from buggies that could charge through the mug

182
00:10:46,801 --> 00:10:49,560
to incredibly fast and powerful buggies

183
00:10:49,561 --> 00:10:51,340
used for racing and mudding events.

184
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:55,500
Today, swamp buggy racing is a popular sport in the South,

185
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:58,520
with three official races held each year.

186
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:04,520
But for these guys,

187
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:08,120
slogging through patches of mud and pools of water

188
00:11:08,121 --> 00:11:10,320
is what swamp living is all about.

189
00:11:13,620 --> 00:11:15,620
I can keep riding all night.

190
00:11:15,621 --> 00:11:16,620
I think he's up for it.

191
00:11:16,621 --> 00:11:17,620
You up for it?

192
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:23,620
If they're lucky, they may spot one of the swamp's

193
00:11:23,621 --> 00:11:27,620
deadliest predators, the American alligator.

194
00:11:27,621 --> 00:11:32,620
Millions of them thrive here, which makes this job

195
00:11:32,621 --> 00:11:33,620
pretty important.

196
00:11:36,620 --> 00:11:40,620
Swamp life can be a blast, but it's also uniquely challenging.

197
00:11:40,621 --> 00:11:45,720
Sometimes the tech that's called for is anything but modern,

198
00:11:45,820 --> 00:11:49,020
especially when dealing with the swamp's deadliest predator,

199
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:50,020
the alligator.

200
00:11:55,960 --> 00:11:59,780
In Florida's Everglades and surrounding swamps,

201
00:11:59,880 --> 00:12:05,020
at least 1 million alligators roam free.

202
00:12:05,120 --> 00:12:08,480
This keeps about three dozen licensed alligator trappers

203
00:12:08,481 --> 00:12:13,480
trappers busy removing gators from residential areas.

204
00:12:13,481 --> 00:12:16,480
Most trappers arm themselves with large shark hooks and

205
00:12:16,481 --> 00:12:20,480
rifles, and the gators they trap are destined for the

206
00:12:20,481 --> 00:12:25,480
processing plant, where they're turned into meat and hides.

207
00:12:25,481 --> 00:12:26,480
Watch out for his head on that.

208
00:12:26,481 --> 00:12:31,480
But these guys do things a bit differently.

209
00:12:31,481 --> 00:12:32,480
What do you want to do?

210
00:12:32,481 --> 00:12:35,480
You want to jump them here or you want to drag them up there?

211
00:12:35,481 --> 00:12:38,480
Licensed trappers Paul Bedard and Jimmy Riffle

212
00:12:38,481 --> 00:12:41,480
prefer a more hands-on approach.

213
00:12:41,481 --> 00:12:45,480
No hooks, no bullets, just muscle and manpower.

214
00:12:45,481 --> 00:12:48,480
The way that Paul and I catch these alligators is very unique.

215
00:12:48,481 --> 00:12:50,480
You won't see many people doing this.

216
00:12:50,481 --> 00:12:52,480
I know a lot of trappers, they all think we're nuts,

217
00:12:52,481 --> 00:12:54,480
but I'd rather take the risk of getting hurt

218
00:12:54,481 --> 00:12:57,480
than have the gator, you know, needlessly get a hook stuck in him

219
00:12:57,481 --> 00:13:00,480
or, you know, God forbid, use a baited shark hook.

220
00:13:00,481 --> 00:13:05,480
It may seem like these guys are just showing off,

221
00:13:05,481 --> 00:13:08,480
but they actually have a loftier goal,

222
00:13:08,481 --> 00:13:12,480
to trap humanely and keep a few more gators alive.

223
00:13:16,480 --> 00:13:18,480
Everyone wants to save the cute fuzzies,

224
00:13:18,481 --> 00:13:20,480
but nobody's out trying to help these guys out,

225
00:13:20,481 --> 00:13:23,480
and they're really incredible animals.

226
00:13:23,481 --> 00:13:25,480
The job requires removing gators

227
00:13:25,481 --> 00:13:29,480
from places they're not wanted to places they are.

228
00:13:29,481 --> 00:13:31,880
Small alligators are taken to a preserve,

229
00:13:31,980 --> 00:13:33,420
where they'll live out their lives.

230
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:36,180
But the big ones, like this nine-foot male,

231
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:39,120
accompany Paul and Jimmy to their other job.

232
00:13:39,220 --> 00:13:40,720
The guys aren't just trappers.

233
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:42,720
Don't you know we're saving your life?

234
00:13:42,820 --> 00:13:44,920
Their real background is in one of the world's

235
00:13:45,020 --> 00:13:47,940
most extreme sports, alligator wrestling.

236
00:13:51,840 --> 00:13:54,000
It's a showcase of trapping techniques

237
00:13:54,100 --> 00:13:57,940
that originated with Florida's Seminole Indians.

238
00:13:57,941 --> 00:13:59,740
Seminoles came up with a technique we now

239
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:01,140
refer to as bulldogging.

240
00:14:04,680 --> 00:14:06,740
Take out your rope.

241
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:10,080
Quick wrap, tie a knot.

242
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:13,460
And you have yourself a cotton-tied alligator.

243
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:15,660
I do want to explain a few things about these alligators

244
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:17,320
that I use in my gator shows.

245
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:18,920
All the gators you see in this pit

246
00:14:19,020 --> 00:14:20,900
are what's known as nuisance alligators.

247
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,100
That means they came out of somebody's backyard, pond,

248
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:26,040
canal, swimming pool, crashed the party, stole the beer,

249
00:14:26,041 --> 00:14:28,340
the beer, ate the dog, something like that.

250
00:14:28,341 --> 00:14:29,920
When that happens, Trapper gets called out.

251
00:14:29,921 --> 00:14:32,840
He catches, removes the alligator, charges you nothing.

252
00:14:32,841 --> 00:14:34,920
Alligator removal's free in the state of Florida

253
00:14:34,921 --> 00:14:36,880
because the alligator is the payment.

254
00:14:36,881 --> 00:14:38,180
When the Trapper gets about 20,

255
00:14:38,181 --> 00:14:39,560
they all go to a processing plant.

256
00:14:39,561 --> 00:14:41,220
So you get alligator belts, boots, handbags,

257
00:14:41,221 --> 00:14:42,760
wallets, things like that.

258
00:14:42,761 --> 00:14:44,600
The lucky ones we buy off the Trapper.

259
00:14:44,601 --> 00:14:47,400
They'll do educational shows at schools, alligator wrestling.

260
00:14:47,401 --> 00:14:49,860
We do a show where we swim with the alligators.

261
00:14:49,861 --> 00:14:51,360
They'll do that kind of stuff anywhere

262
00:14:51,361 --> 00:14:53,940
between six months to two years.

263
00:14:53,941 --> 00:14:56,640
It is never legal to release them back in the wild.

264
00:14:56,740 --> 00:14:58,220
It's absolutely forbidden by law.

265
00:14:58,720 --> 00:15:00,180
So we work with a few different preserves,

266
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:02,000
largest being about 2,500 acres,

267
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:03,720
but it is enclosed in a fence.

268
00:15:04,140 --> 00:15:05,480
Only because of that perimeter fence

269
00:15:05,481 --> 00:15:06,820
are we allowed to release them out there,

270
00:15:07,160 --> 00:15:09,300
basically giving these guys a wild existence again.

271
00:15:09,800 --> 00:15:11,640
We saved, I think, 16 last year.

272
00:15:12,060 --> 00:15:13,560
State of Florida killed over 10,000.

273
00:15:14,220 --> 00:15:16,180
We don't make a huge difference, nor do I claim to.

274
00:15:16,460 --> 00:15:17,460
Obviously, it makes a big difference

275
00:15:17,461 --> 00:15:18,640
to each gator you see in this pit.

276
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:21,700
This pit is where Paul and Jimmy's trapping skills

277
00:15:21,701 --> 00:15:22,940
are honed and perfected.

278
00:15:23,940 --> 00:15:24,840
to a point.

279
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:47,300
It's enough of that stuff.

280
00:15:47,301 --> 00:15:50,340
I've been bit 19 times where a gator has actually locked on

281
00:15:50,341 --> 00:15:52,700
or bit and released or got a couple of teeth in me.

282
00:15:52,701 --> 00:15:55,700
I've had a 12-foot gator lock onto my hand.

283
00:15:55,701 --> 00:15:58,700
I've been grazed probably another 10 or 15 times.

284
00:15:58,701 --> 00:16:01,700
Put one tooth and rip a hole in my arm about that big.

285
00:16:01,701 --> 00:16:04,700
But, I mean, that's not that bad.

286
00:16:04,701 --> 00:16:07,700
Scars are part of the job description,

287
00:16:07,701 --> 00:16:10,700
but needless risk-taking is not.

288
00:16:13,700 --> 00:16:15,700
Paul and Jimmy work with their gators frequently,

289
00:16:15,701 --> 00:16:17,700
allowing them to grow accustomed to humans

290
00:16:17,701 --> 00:16:19,700
before putting them in the pit.

291
00:16:22,700 --> 00:16:35,100
Trapping, however, is another story. Wild alligators are not to be underestimated.

292
00:16:35,101 --> 00:16:39,980
They're often found cooling off in bodies of water. Residential swimming pools, for

293
00:16:39,981 --> 00:16:48,640
example. Florida's tropical climate means Paul and Jimmy make plenty of house calls.

294
00:16:48,641 --> 00:16:52,380
But residential trapping is just one part of the job.

295
00:16:52,381 --> 00:16:55,180
Nuisance gators could be lurking anywhere.

296
00:16:55,181 --> 00:16:56,780
Well, if the water's clear, I'll jump in the water,

297
00:16:56,781 --> 00:16:59,280
and he's usually hanging under the bank.

298
00:16:59,281 --> 00:17:02,280
They make these little dens in the side of the canals,

299
00:17:02,281 --> 00:17:03,280
the ponds.

300
00:17:03,281 --> 00:17:04,780
They just go in, they'll look you right in the face,

301
00:17:04,781 --> 00:17:06,500
and they're very placid underwater.

302
00:17:06,501 --> 00:17:09,200
And you can just gently put a noose around his neck

303
00:17:09,201 --> 00:17:12,700
and pull him out.

304
00:17:12,701 --> 00:17:16,700
The noose allows Paul to pull the animal onto land.

305
00:17:18,640 --> 00:17:21,640
But in shallow water, Paul discards the noose

306
00:17:21,740 --> 00:17:24,640
in favor of this nerve-wracking approach.

307
00:17:24,740 --> 00:17:26,640
There's a certain dance you can do with him

308
00:17:26,740 --> 00:17:28,640
where you get on kind of his perimeter,

309
00:17:28,740 --> 00:17:30,080
and you kind of get a feel for him.

310
00:17:30,180 --> 00:17:32,080
You try to levitate him by picking up his jaw,

311
00:17:32,180 --> 00:17:33,380
and he'll back away.

312
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:34,880
And do it again, he'll back away.

313
00:17:34,980 --> 00:17:37,000
But if you're going to pull him out of a pool or something,

314
00:17:37,100 --> 00:17:38,900
you want to get him in a position where what I like to do

315
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,400
is get him over my head and get my head underneath his front

316
00:17:42,500 --> 00:17:43,400
leg.

317
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:46,000
And then once I get him there, I got to grab him and lock up.

318
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:48,600
But you got to go quick, because in that little bit,

319
00:17:48,640 --> 00:17:50,980
little bit of time where you're sliding your hands over,

320
00:17:50,981 --> 00:17:54,140
if he decides to come back, he can spin and bite your head off.

321
00:17:54,141 --> 00:17:56,240
Sometimes conditions are too risky,

322
00:17:56,241 --> 00:17:59,360
even for these guys.

323
00:17:59,361 --> 00:18:02,920
If the water is murky or an alligator is too aggressive,

324
00:18:02,921 --> 00:18:06,120
hands-on trapping isn't very wise.

325
00:18:06,121 --> 00:18:08,120
For the most dangerous situations,

326
00:18:08,121 --> 00:18:11,760
Paul created a contraption that not only traps gators,

327
00:18:11,761 --> 00:18:14,160
but keeps them unharmed.

328
00:18:14,161 --> 00:18:16,740
It's just a basic live caught gator trap.

329
00:18:16,741 --> 00:18:18,100
What a lot of the trappers use,

330
00:18:18,200 --> 00:18:19,640
if they get a real spooky, shy gator,

331
00:18:19,940 --> 00:18:21,320
they put a baited shark hook out.

332
00:18:21,580 --> 00:18:23,360
He swallows the shark hook, he gets it in his stomach,

333
00:18:23,460 --> 00:18:26,080
he spends all night pulling on it, it rips up his insides.

334
00:18:26,520 --> 00:18:29,360
So what we did is I came up with this gator trap.

335
00:18:29,440 --> 00:18:31,920
It's basically a copy of a bear trap.

336
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:34,700
And just put pontoons on it to float it.

337
00:18:34,780 --> 00:18:36,120
It's a culvert pipe, it's plastic,

338
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:37,260
he won't get beat up inside.

339
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:39,020
He goes in, he pulls the bait down,

340
00:18:39,080 --> 00:18:40,160
the door drops behind him,

341
00:18:40,580 --> 00:18:41,960
and you get a live caught gator.

342
00:18:41,961 --> 00:18:43,800
It floats so he's got enough air to breathe.

343
00:18:44,720 --> 00:18:46,380
The bait is usually chicken,

344
00:18:46,381 --> 00:18:48,880
of meat that's been sitting out for a few days.

345
00:18:48,980 --> 00:18:51,120
Alligators like their meat well seasoned.

346
00:18:53,320 --> 00:18:56,420
It takes about 20 pounds of pressure on the gator's part

347
00:18:56,520 --> 00:18:59,020
to pull the bait and spring the trap.

348
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:03,400
But for a 400-pound reptile, 20 pounds of pressure is nothing.

349
00:19:09,100 --> 00:19:11,640
Alligator trapping slows down in the winter.

350
00:19:11,740 --> 00:19:16,380
These reptiles often go dormant when the mercury drops below 55.

351
00:19:16,381 --> 00:19:19,980
And today, it's a chilly 50 degrees.

352
00:19:20,080 --> 00:19:22,860
Still, Paul and Jimmy agreed to give modern marvels

353
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:25,560
an in-depth, step-by-step demonstration

354
00:19:25,660 --> 00:19:27,660
of their underwater trapping techniques.

355
00:19:34,760 --> 00:19:38,460
The plan is to use two alligators from the wrestling pit.

356
00:19:38,560 --> 00:19:41,440
Paul will work with one, while the second is kept warm

357
00:19:41,540 --> 00:19:43,100
in a heated box.

358
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:45,100
Cameras above and below the water

359
00:19:45,101 --> 00:19:46,900
will capture the action.

360
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,900
But the minute the guys put gator number one in the water,

361
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,300
their plans begin to fall apart.

362
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:53,300
You see him?

363
00:19:53,400 --> 00:19:54,300
No, he's gone.

364
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:55,100
Let's get out of here.

365
00:19:57,840 --> 00:20:00,840
The water in this pond is about 45 degrees,

366
00:20:00,940 --> 00:20:03,160
too chilly for the alligator's comfort.

367
00:20:03,260 --> 00:20:05,460
Looking for warmth, he quickly swims

368
00:20:05,560 --> 00:20:07,560
to a big patch of weeds in the middle

369
00:20:07,660 --> 00:20:09,100
and disappears from sight.

370
00:20:12,400 --> 00:20:15,060
Even with Paul's experience, going into the water

371
00:20:15,100 --> 00:20:18,640
an invisible gator is extremely risky.

372
00:20:18,740 --> 00:20:20,540
Pretty murky.

373
00:20:20,640 --> 00:20:23,600
But unfortunately, Paul has to go in.

374
00:20:23,700 --> 00:20:25,440
It's the only way to recapture it.

375
00:20:25,540 --> 00:20:27,940
He's got to be hiding in this weed over here.

376
00:20:28,040 --> 00:20:31,340
So we poked around with a pole trying to see if we can find him.

377
00:20:31,440 --> 00:20:34,460
We saw something move, but he's not moving.

378
00:20:34,560 --> 00:20:36,260
No, just kidding.

379
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:38,360
He's not coming out of that weed, so I'm going to have to go in

380
00:20:38,460 --> 00:20:41,000
and try to find him again, let that silt settle down a little

381
00:20:41,100 --> 00:20:44,500
bit, and then just poke around with your hands till you find

382
00:20:44,501 --> 00:20:46,500
and hopefully you find them.

383
00:20:46,501 --> 00:20:48,500
And hopefully you find the right end of the gator.

384
00:20:53,500 --> 00:20:55,500
Watch out for his head on that nail right there.

385
00:20:55,501 --> 00:20:56,500
Here we go.

386
00:20:59,500 --> 00:21:02,500
Paul Bedard and Jimmy Ripple are two of the most unique

387
00:21:02,501 --> 00:21:04,500
alligator trappers in the state of Florida.

388
00:21:04,501 --> 00:21:08,500
They rely on just their hands and a minimal amount of gear

389
00:21:08,501 --> 00:21:11,500
to remove nuisance gators from residential areas.

390
00:21:14,500 --> 00:21:16,500
We're probably the only trappers that use this technique,

391
00:21:16,501 --> 00:21:19,500
as far as I know, but we come from a different background.

392
00:21:19,501 --> 00:21:21,500
You know, we come from an alligator wrestling background

393
00:21:21,501 --> 00:21:23,500
where we learned how to handle the animal hands-on,

394
00:21:23,501 --> 00:21:26,500
not with lines and hooks and all the other stuff.

395
00:21:26,501 --> 00:21:29,500
Today, the guy's skills will be put to the test

396
00:21:29,501 --> 00:21:33,500
as a trapping demonstration prepared for modern Marvel's cameras

397
00:21:33,501 --> 00:21:35,500
begins to unravel.

398
00:21:35,501 --> 00:21:38,500
The object of the demo is a nine-foot alligator.

399
00:21:38,501 --> 00:21:40,500
Paul was going to work with him underwater,

400
00:21:40,501 --> 00:21:42,500
but the water is cold,

401
00:21:42,501 --> 00:21:46,100
and the gator has buried himself deep in the mud.

402
00:21:46,200 --> 00:21:49,600
Paul is forced to do the one thing he would usually avoid,

403
00:21:49,700 --> 00:21:53,840
search for a hidden alligator.

404
00:21:53,940 --> 00:21:57,240
Alligators can stay underwater for long periods of time,

405
00:21:57,340 --> 00:22:01,260
up to several hours if conditions are right.

406
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:04,560
And this gator seems to have burrowed in.

407
00:22:04,660 --> 00:22:07,560
Paul can't tell the alligator's tail from his mouth,

408
00:22:07,660 --> 00:22:12,500
and it's too dangerous to risk touching the wrong end.

409
00:22:12,501 --> 00:22:16,500
You can't see anything down there.

410
00:22:16,501 --> 00:22:19,540
Frustrated, the guys decide to leave the first alligator

411
00:22:19,541 --> 00:22:25,180
alone and put the second one in, hoping to get the shot.

412
00:22:25,181 --> 00:22:27,880
This time, however, they keep a lariat around his neck

413
00:22:27,881 --> 00:22:29,620
so they don't lose him.

414
00:22:29,621 --> 00:22:31,360
Hey, remember the rule.

415
00:22:31,361 --> 00:22:34,660
Do not move.

416
00:22:34,661 --> 00:22:36,560
But losing this gator could be the least

417
00:22:36,561 --> 00:22:38,720
of their concerns.

418
00:22:38,721 --> 00:22:41,200
Oh, by the way, there's another gator in here.

419
00:22:41,201 --> 00:22:42,440
Yeah, don't forget about that.

420
00:22:48,700 --> 00:22:51,680
Paul moves to a clear spot for his trapping demo.

421
00:22:51,780 --> 00:22:55,080
He has to keep a sharp eye on his alligator, a difficult task

422
00:22:55,180 --> 00:22:56,180
in water this murky.

423
00:23:01,420 --> 00:23:03,660
As Paul fights to manage his gator,

424
00:23:03,760 --> 00:23:06,960
Jimmy sees something that makes him nervous.

425
00:23:07,060 --> 00:23:08,320
Hey, give me that little lariat.

426
00:23:08,420 --> 00:23:10,660
Bubbles on the surface mean the first alligator

427
00:23:10,661 --> 00:23:13,960
is coming up, it's exactly what the guys did not want to happen.

428
00:23:14,240 --> 00:23:17,900
If either alligator gets spooked, things could go horribly wrong.

429
00:23:17,901 --> 00:23:19,100
Paul? Paul?

430
00:23:19,660 --> 00:23:21,580
Jimmy struggles to get his attention.

431
00:23:22,140 --> 00:23:23,200
We're done. Hang on.

432
00:23:23,740 --> 00:23:26,340
But Paul is intensely focused on his alligator

433
00:23:26,341 --> 00:23:28,300
and isn't aware of the situation.

434
00:23:28,380 --> 00:23:29,340
Listen. Paul? Paul?

435
00:23:29,880 --> 00:23:31,500
Don't swim over here, Paul. Hang on.

436
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:34,240
He's right on the side here. He's coming up for air.

437
00:23:34,640 --> 00:23:35,980
As soon as he comes up... Coming up right now.

438
00:23:40,660 --> 00:23:45,660
Luckily, the alligator swims toward the water's edge and

439
00:23:45,760 --> 00:23:47,660
right into Jimmy's lariat.

440
00:23:47,760 --> 00:23:52,600
He's cold, lethargic, and covered in mud.

441
00:23:52,700 --> 00:23:55,600
It's no wonder he was invisible.

442
00:23:55,700 --> 00:23:56,600
Mike, come on.

443
00:23:56,700 --> 00:23:58,120
I got some.

444
00:23:58,220 --> 00:23:59,620
Bring him out.

445
00:23:59,720 --> 00:24:00,620
Bring him out.

446
00:24:04,620 --> 00:24:08,620
A quick wrap with electrical tape secures the gator's jaws.

447
00:24:08,621 --> 00:24:10,120
with his jaws.

448
00:24:12,620 --> 00:24:14,720
Paul, meanwhile, has had enough.

449
00:24:14,820 --> 00:24:17,560
He and Jimmy haul the second alligator out of the water

450
00:24:17,660 --> 00:24:18,860
and call it a day.

451
00:24:22,460 --> 00:24:24,940
While the trapping demo didn't go as planned...

452
00:24:25,040 --> 00:24:26,300
You said you got him. I looked up.

453
00:24:26,400 --> 00:24:28,680
I was like, yeah, you got him because he's right in front of me.

454
00:24:28,780 --> 00:24:32,280
Reality is always unpredictable and often more exciting.

455
00:24:32,380 --> 00:24:34,980
It's real murky down there, huh?

456
00:24:35,080 --> 00:24:36,780
And you are freezing.

457
00:24:36,781 --> 00:24:38,140
in the 30s the last three days.

458
00:24:38,220 --> 00:24:38,920
This is ridiculous.

459
00:24:40,080 --> 00:24:42,180
Paul and Jimmy will take these two alligators

460
00:24:42,181 --> 00:24:43,540
back to a temporary home

461
00:24:43,541 --> 00:24:46,420
while they continue searching for a permanent place

462
00:24:46,421 --> 00:24:47,121
for them to live.

463
00:24:47,780 --> 00:24:49,560
We'll use them for alligator wrestling shows

464
00:24:49,561 --> 00:24:50,300
for a year or two.

465
00:24:50,700 --> 00:24:52,340
Then we'll try to find zoos or sanctuaries

466
00:24:52,341 --> 00:24:53,041
to donate them to.

467
00:24:53,460 --> 00:24:54,700
Anywhere, somebody will take them

468
00:24:54,701 --> 00:24:55,640
and they're not going to, you know,

469
00:24:56,020 --> 00:24:57,080
turn them into a suitcase.

470
00:24:57,500 --> 00:24:59,080
You know, we'd rather have that happen to them.

471
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:00,780
For Paul and Jimmy,

472
00:25:01,340 --> 00:25:03,500
protecting alligators is a lifelong quest.

473
00:25:04,300 --> 00:25:06,560
They are, after all, the Swamp Star Residence.

474
00:25:06,780 --> 00:25:08,780
and one of its top predators.

475
00:25:12,780 --> 00:25:14,980
Nearby in South Florida's Everglades,

476
00:25:15,020 --> 00:25:17,300
another team is also on a quest.

477
00:25:17,320 --> 00:25:21,420
But for them, eradication of a dangerous invasive carnivore

478
00:25:21,460 --> 00:25:23,020
is the goal.

479
00:25:23,060 --> 00:25:26,260
The invaders are Burmese pythons,

480
00:25:26,300 --> 00:25:29,300
one of the largest snake species on the planet.

481
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:40,780
Pythons are an exotic invasive in Florida.

482
00:25:40,781 --> 00:25:42,280
They don't belong here.

483
00:25:42,281 --> 00:25:44,280
They're very well adapted to Florida.

484
00:25:44,281 --> 00:25:45,780
They grow quickly.

485
00:25:45,781 --> 00:25:48,280
They multiply in large numbers.

486
00:25:48,281 --> 00:25:51,280
Females can have up to 100 eggs when they're full grown.

487
00:25:51,281 --> 00:25:54,280
Burmese pythons are indigenous to Southeast Asia.

488
00:25:54,281 --> 00:25:57,280
The snakes in Florida could be the descendants of released

489
00:25:57,281 --> 00:26:00,780
pets, courtesy of overwhelmed owners.

490
00:26:00,781 --> 00:26:03,780
Another theory blames hurricanes for destroying animal

491
00:26:03,781 --> 00:26:04,780
warehouses.

492
00:26:04,781 --> 00:26:08,280
Whatever the cause, Florida's python population

493
00:26:08,281 --> 00:26:12,420
has exploded from just a few to tens of thousands.

494
00:26:12,421 --> 00:26:16,420
If left unchecked, experts fear they could spread,

495
00:26:16,421 --> 00:26:20,920
possibly as far north as Washington, D.C.

496
00:26:20,921 --> 00:26:25,040
But scientists are on the case with a high-tech research project

497
00:26:25,041 --> 00:26:28,480
aimed at serious population control.

498
00:26:28,481 --> 00:26:31,980
We're trying to figure out the best way to go about,

499
00:26:31,981 --> 00:26:33,740
you know, getting rid of these things.

500
00:26:33,741 --> 00:26:35,980
And in order to learn that, we have to know some basic

501
00:26:36,080 --> 00:26:39,980
information about what time of day they're active, when is the

502
00:26:40,080 --> 00:26:43,980
best time to go out and look for snakes to capture them.

503
00:26:44,080 --> 00:26:46,280
Gathering this data is difficult.

504
00:26:46,380 --> 00:26:49,780
Pythons are elusive and hard to observe over time.

505
00:26:49,880 --> 00:26:52,300
But in this tiny, cutting-edge device,

506
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:55,600
scientists finally have an ace up their sleeve.

507
00:26:55,700 --> 00:26:58,200
It's called an accelerometer.

508
00:26:58,300 --> 00:27:00,140
Think of it as a digital spy.

509
00:27:00,141 --> 00:27:03,440
It's a device that records minute details about motion.

510
00:27:03,441 --> 00:27:05,940
The accelerometers in our tags can tell us when the animals

511
00:27:05,941 --> 00:27:09,740
are moving or when they're resting or when they're, in some

512
00:27:09,741 --> 00:27:13,740
cases, mating, in some cases, feeding.

513
00:27:13,741 --> 00:27:16,540
The accelerometer will work in conjunction with a radio

514
00:27:16,541 --> 00:27:18,940
transmitter and GPS.

515
00:27:18,941 --> 00:27:21,940
The GPS records the snake's location.

516
00:27:21,941 --> 00:27:25,540
The radio transmitter allows scientists to track their snakes

517
00:27:25,541 --> 00:27:29,940
and find them later, a crucial detail.

518
00:27:29,941 --> 00:27:33,100
These tags will generate 10 million data points per day,

519
00:27:33,440 --> 00:27:35,980
and that's too much data to actually be transmitted,

520
00:27:36,260 --> 00:27:39,800
so we have to actually get this tag back from the animal to get our data.

521
00:27:40,540 --> 00:27:42,700
But the first step is to implant the tags.

522
00:27:43,820 --> 00:27:46,760
Today, scientists will implant two large pythons

523
00:27:46,761 --> 00:27:48,880
that were recently caught in the Everglades.

524
00:27:49,980 --> 00:27:52,600
What we're going to do is take the snake out of the box outside,

525
00:27:52,760 --> 00:27:54,060
so if she has to go to the bathroom or anything,

526
00:27:54,160 --> 00:27:56,500
that's done on the grass and not in the lab.

527
00:27:56,501 --> 00:28:01,500
Python 41 is a 13-foot female that weighs about 100 pounds.

528
00:28:09,740 --> 00:28:15,260
And this is Python 42, a smaller but feistier specimen.

529
00:28:21,260 --> 00:28:24,060
Once their business is done, both snakes are carted off

530
00:28:24,061 --> 00:28:26,600
Both snakes are carted off to the lab, where they'll be

531
00:28:26,700 --> 00:28:29,240
anesthetized using a combination of gases

532
00:28:29,340 --> 00:28:30,940
specifically designed for pythons.

533
00:28:37,040 --> 00:28:40,180
The anesthesia is delivered through a tracheal tube,

534
00:28:40,280 --> 00:28:41,880
a very common veterinary procedure.

535
00:28:45,580 --> 00:28:48,480
Modern Marvels was asked not to film the surgery,

536
00:28:48,580 --> 00:28:50,960
but the procedure itself is simple.

537
00:28:51,060 --> 00:28:54,060
The tags will be sutured into the tail region of the snake.

538
00:28:54,061 --> 00:28:57,020
where they'll cause minimal discomfort.

539
00:28:57,021 --> 00:28:59,540
The whole process takes just under an hour,

540
00:28:59,541 --> 00:29:01,100
and the pythons recover quickly.

541
00:29:04,600 --> 00:29:06,740
The next day, they're ready to be released back

542
00:29:06,741 --> 00:29:10,680
into the wild, where they'll resume normal life.

543
00:29:10,681 --> 00:29:12,440
Python 41 is first.

544
00:29:14,940 --> 00:29:15,840
All right, this looks good.

545
00:29:19,520 --> 00:29:22,460
The snake is gently rolled out, belly up.

546
00:29:22,461 --> 00:29:25,020
This will help scientists calibrate the orientation

547
00:29:25,021 --> 00:29:27,300
of their tags.

548
00:29:27,301 --> 00:29:29,240
When they look at the data that comes back,

549
00:29:29,241 --> 00:29:33,580
they'll know which side is up and which is down.

550
00:29:33,581 --> 00:29:39,680
So it's going to be waypoint number 386.

551
00:29:39,681 --> 00:29:41,180
They're right back here and you hear that sound.

552
00:29:45,340 --> 00:29:49,360
This process is repeated with Python 42.

553
00:29:49,361 --> 00:29:52,320
You can see the GPS antenna sticking out

554
00:29:52,321 --> 00:29:54,320
Yeah, looks like it's good.

555
00:30:05,200 --> 00:30:07,200
Just pull the box back.

556
00:30:07,201 --> 00:30:10,200
We'll track Python 42 for the next four months.

557
00:30:10,201 --> 00:30:12,280
That's the battery life and the accelerometer

558
00:30:12,281 --> 00:30:13,880
and the GPS transmitter.

559
00:30:13,881 --> 00:30:15,880
So we'll track her weekly from the air,

560
00:30:15,881 --> 00:30:17,320
and then we'll walk in on her weekly

561
00:30:17,321 --> 00:30:19,320
to see if we can find other males with her.

562
00:30:22,320 --> 00:30:29,200
Invasive species are a devastating swamp problem,

563
00:30:29,201 --> 00:30:31,200
and in South Florida's Everglades,

564
00:30:31,320 --> 00:30:33,460
a huge invasion of Burmese pythons

565
00:30:33,461 --> 00:30:34,780
could be getting out of hand.

566
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:37,440
A research project is underway

567
00:30:37,441 --> 00:30:39,620
that may shed light on these elusive snakes

568
00:30:39,621 --> 00:30:42,300
and help get their numbers under control.

569
00:30:44,660 --> 00:30:46,620
Scientists are using high-tech tags

570
00:30:46,621 --> 00:30:48,200
implanted in several pythons

571
00:30:48,201 --> 00:30:50,040
to learn where the snakes go,

572
00:30:50,560 --> 00:30:52,020
as well as what they do

573
00:30:52,021 --> 00:30:53,020
when they get there.

574
00:30:56,320 --> 00:30:57,460
How often do they feed?

575
00:30:57,461 --> 00:30:59,460
How many animals are they actually eating

576
00:30:59,461 --> 00:31:01,860
over the course of a month or two months?

577
00:31:01,861 --> 00:31:04,300
All of this helps us to not only figure out

578
00:31:04,301 --> 00:31:05,900
the best way to get rid of these snakes,

579
00:31:05,901 --> 00:31:08,440
but also helps us quantify the actual impact

580
00:31:08,441 --> 00:31:09,880
they're having on the ecosystem.

581
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:14,040
Tracking tagged pythons by air

582
00:31:14,041 --> 00:31:16,880
is the best way to find them in Florida's vast Everglades.

583
00:31:16,881 --> 00:31:17,880
There you go.

584
00:31:17,881 --> 00:31:23,880
Let's go find some snakes here.

585
00:31:23,881 --> 00:31:24,880
Yeah.

586
00:31:24,881 --> 00:31:29,260
The plane is rigged with two antennae, one on each wing.

587
00:31:29,261 --> 00:31:33,480
This allows researchers to pick up signals from the radio transmitters and determine

588
00:31:33,481 --> 00:31:35,480
which direction they're coming from.

589
00:31:35,481 --> 00:31:40,480
Yeah, it sounds like it might be right on that tree island down there.

590
00:31:40,481 --> 00:31:41,480
Yep, there it is.

591
00:31:41,481 --> 00:31:42,480
Yep.

592
00:31:42,481 --> 00:31:44,480
Right in the middle of that pond.

593
00:31:44,481 --> 00:31:45,480
Yeah.

594
00:31:45,481 --> 00:31:47,980
is also a bird record.

595
00:31:47,981 --> 00:31:50,720
Can we imagine how it's being down there?

596
00:31:50,721 --> 00:31:51,421
Yeah.

597
00:31:51,422 --> 00:31:52,122
It's hot.

598
00:31:52,123 --> 00:31:53,080
Yeah.

599
00:31:53,081 --> 00:31:55,480
The weather has turned cold, so snakes

600
00:31:55,481 --> 00:31:57,080
aren't moving very far.

601
00:31:57,081 --> 00:31:58,220
And why would they?

602
00:31:58,221 --> 00:32:01,080
These unwanted guests have settled down to a buffet

603
00:32:01,081 --> 00:32:02,980
in a bird colony.

604
00:32:02,981 --> 00:32:05,540
It's a disturbing reminder of the devastation caused

605
00:32:05,541 --> 00:32:08,380
by pythons in the Everglades.

606
00:32:08,381 --> 00:32:11,780
The snake's location is just one layer of information.

607
00:32:11,781 --> 00:32:14,520
Biologists will also go in on foot,

608
00:32:14,521 --> 00:32:17,280
hoping to determine if their implanted snakes are breeding.

609
00:32:21,360 --> 00:32:22,060
I don't know.

610
00:32:22,061 --> 00:32:24,260
It kind of sounds like she's still in that tree line.

611
00:32:24,261 --> 00:32:27,660
Yeah, yeah, there I see her.

612
00:32:27,661 --> 00:32:31,280
But the real results come when the snakes are recaptured

613
00:32:31,281 --> 00:32:34,280
and the data is downloaded.

614
00:32:34,281 --> 00:32:36,300
It will look something like this.

615
00:32:36,301 --> 00:32:39,480
Not much to a layperson, but to a scientist,

616
00:32:39,481 --> 00:32:41,720
it's a treasure trove of information

617
00:32:41,721 --> 00:32:46,020
that allows them to recreate a day in the life of a python.

618
00:32:46,021 --> 00:32:50,160
This is data from about a 13-foot female snake

619
00:32:50,161 --> 00:32:52,400
that was released into the Everglades.

620
00:32:52,401 --> 00:32:54,700
And then we got her back and got the tag

621
00:32:54,701 --> 00:32:56,240
and recovered our data.

622
00:32:56,241 --> 00:32:59,540
And what we're seeing here is these dark bars represent

623
00:32:59,541 --> 00:33:02,140
nighttime, and the white is daytime.

624
00:33:02,141 --> 00:33:04,740
The main point here is that when you see these big spikes

625
00:33:04,741 --> 00:33:08,680
like this, this is moving, the animals covering ground.

626
00:33:08,681 --> 00:33:11,040
And when you see these basic flat lines,

627
00:33:11,041 --> 00:33:13,340
That means the animal's not moving.

628
00:33:13,341 --> 00:33:15,980
Pythons generally do a lot of lying around,

629
00:33:15,981 --> 00:33:19,380
especially during the heat of the day.

630
00:33:19,381 --> 00:33:22,680
But this python apparently got hungry.

631
00:33:22,681 --> 00:33:24,180
We've got three flat lines here.

632
00:33:24,181 --> 00:33:27,080
The animal's just resting throughout the night.

633
00:33:27,081 --> 00:33:29,300
And at about 7.45 in the morning, all of a sudden,

634
00:33:29,301 --> 00:33:31,000
we have a major disturbance.

635
00:33:31,001 --> 00:33:34,800
What we think is that the animal was resting,

636
00:33:34,801 --> 00:33:36,540
waiting for something to stumble upon it,

637
00:33:36,541 --> 00:33:39,000
and then ambushed something right here.

638
00:33:39,001 --> 00:33:43,140
And what they do is strike and then coil and constrict

639
00:33:43,141 --> 00:33:44,340
on the animal.

640
00:33:44,341 --> 00:33:48,320
And we think that's what we're seeing from this data.

641
00:33:48,321 --> 00:33:51,540
Scientists know that a complete eradication of pythons

642
00:33:51,541 --> 00:33:54,420
is unlikely.

643
00:33:54,421 --> 00:33:56,240
But the data they're collecting will

644
00:33:56,241 --> 00:33:59,560
allow them to come up with ways to remove as many as possible.

645
00:33:59,561 --> 00:34:03,360
And every snake removed is one less to breed, hunt,

646
00:34:03,361 --> 00:34:07,020
and harm this delicate ecosystem.

647
00:34:07,021 --> 00:34:09,880
Invasive species are so harmful, in part

648
00:34:09,881 --> 00:34:12,260
because they eat the swamp clean.

649
00:34:12,261 --> 00:34:14,900
They usually have no natural predators.

650
00:34:14,901 --> 00:34:17,740
But that isn't the case everywhere.

651
00:34:17,741 --> 00:34:21,580
In Louisiana's Atchafalaya, millions of oraceous rodents,

652
00:34:21,581 --> 00:34:25,060
called nutria, are running amok, literally devouring

653
00:34:25,061 --> 00:34:25,780
the wetlands.

654
00:34:28,560 --> 00:34:31,700
They weigh about 15 pounds, and their eating habits

655
00:34:31,701 --> 00:34:34,800
are exposing the swamp soil to erosion.

656
00:34:34,801 --> 00:34:37,420
This in turn increases the risk of flooding.

657
00:34:39,380 --> 00:34:43,080
Nutria are devastating, but here in Cajun country,

658
00:34:43,081 --> 00:34:47,140
they happen to have an unexpected predator, man.

659
00:34:49,440 --> 00:34:51,840
Trampers harvest thousands of nutria each year

660
00:34:51,841 --> 00:34:53,080
from the swamp.

661
00:34:53,081 --> 00:34:55,660
Right now, it's the only form of population control.

662
00:34:57,560 --> 00:34:59,960
Rather than let the rodents go to waste,

663
00:34:59,961 --> 00:35:02,200
several prominent Louisiana chefs

664
00:35:02,201 --> 00:35:05,360
are incorporating nutria into their recipes.

665
00:35:05,361 --> 00:35:08,140
I've started cooking with all of the exotic game

666
00:35:08,141 --> 00:35:10,540
of the bayou when I was eight, nine, 10 years old,

667
00:35:10,541 --> 00:35:12,600
living in the swamps of Louisiana,

668
00:35:12,601 --> 00:35:14,600
this being our swamp floor pantry.

669
00:35:14,601 --> 00:35:16,880
This was our grocery store.

670
00:35:16,881 --> 00:35:19,420
John Foltz is an award-winning chef

671
00:35:19,421 --> 00:35:22,940
who prides himself on cooking whatever the land has to offer

672
00:35:22,941 --> 00:35:25,440
with plenty of Cajun flair.

673
00:35:25,441 --> 00:35:27,820
This is a nutria sauce pecan.

674
00:35:27,821 --> 00:35:29,520
Sauce pecan meaning hot and spicy

675
00:35:29,521 --> 00:35:33,060
from the Spanish culture of Louisiana,

676
00:35:33,061 --> 00:35:36,400
using what many people in America might consider

677
00:35:36,401 --> 00:35:39,240
a nuisance animal.

678
00:35:39,241 --> 00:35:42,900
Nutria, one of these animals that were brought to Louisiana

679
00:35:42,901 --> 00:35:45,740
many years ago from South America for the fur crop.

680
00:35:45,741 --> 00:35:47,940
It was a pelt business.

681
00:35:47,941 --> 00:35:49,580
And eventually a hurricane came in,

682
00:35:49,581 --> 00:35:51,540
destroyed the pins of the nutria.

683
00:35:51,541 --> 00:35:53,080
They got out into the swamplands,

684
00:35:53,081 --> 00:35:56,440
and of course immediately found it a thriving place to live in.

685
00:35:56,441 --> 00:35:59,280
And of course, with six or seven little nutria

686
00:35:59,281 --> 00:36:01,940
in every litter and a couple of litters a year,

687
00:36:01,941 --> 00:36:03,920
we have a lot of nutria here.

688
00:36:03,921 --> 00:36:06,480
Nutria are strict vegetarians,

689
00:36:06,481 --> 00:36:08,880
what chefs call clean animals.

690
00:36:08,881 --> 00:36:11,960
This not only makes them safe for human consumption,

691
00:36:11,961 --> 00:36:16,260
according to many believers, it also makes them delicious.

692
00:36:16,261 --> 00:36:20,260
And that's gonna go into the dark brown roux as well.

693
00:36:20,261 --> 00:36:22,500
Even though the animal is a little difficult

694
00:36:22,501 --> 00:36:24,600
to get past the look once you get to the meat,

695
00:36:24,601 --> 00:36:25,900
it's absolutely beautiful.

696
00:36:25,901 --> 00:36:27,900
It's a lot like Team Rabbit almost,

697
00:36:27,901 --> 00:36:31,900
and it adapts very, very well to any type of cooking style.

698
00:36:35,900 --> 00:36:38,900
And this is what it looks like when it's done.

699
00:36:41,900 --> 00:36:43,900
See how tender that is?

700
00:36:48,900 --> 00:36:50,900
It's like I cooked it myself.

701
00:36:50,901 --> 00:36:52,900
Oh, doesn't it look good?

702
00:36:52,901 --> 00:36:53,900
Thank you.

703
00:36:53,901 --> 00:36:56,700
None of this is strange to the Louisianian,

704
00:36:56,701 --> 00:36:58,900
especially when you come from a trapper's family,

705
00:36:58,901 --> 00:36:59,900
the way I did.

706
00:36:59,901 --> 00:37:03,400
So anything, if it crawled, if it swam, if it flew,

707
00:37:03,401 --> 00:37:04,900
if it had feathers or fur on it,

708
00:37:04,901 --> 00:37:06,900
it was destined for the cast iron pot.

709
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:13,900
It's a novel solution for a common swamp problem.

710
00:37:13,901 --> 00:37:16,900
But regrettably, we can't eat our way out of everything

711
00:37:16,901 --> 00:37:18,900
Mother Nature cooks up.

712
00:37:19,900 --> 00:37:22,900
Sometimes, a massive engineering project is called for.

713
00:37:23,900 --> 00:37:31,080
Swamps are one of the world's most unique places,

714
00:37:31,400 --> 00:37:33,680
but the same rivers that feed these wetlands

715
00:37:33,681 --> 00:37:36,680
and bring them to life can also overpower them.

716
00:37:38,580 --> 00:37:42,280
Flooding is a common and dangerous swamp problem,

717
00:37:42,920 --> 00:37:45,660
especially in Louisiana's Atchafalaya Basin.

718
00:37:47,460 --> 00:37:49,640
The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers

719
00:37:49,641 --> 00:37:51,780
has been working on a flood control solution

720
00:37:51,781 --> 00:37:52,940
for more than a century,

721
00:37:52,941 --> 00:37:54,940
And in this massive engineering project,

722
00:37:54,941 --> 00:37:56,940
they may have found it.

723
00:37:56,941 --> 00:37:58,940
It's called revetment.

724
00:37:58,941 --> 00:38:01,940
It's basically a method of reinforcing the banks of a river,

725
00:38:01,941 --> 00:38:05,940
in this case, the mighty Mississippi.

726
00:38:05,941 --> 00:38:08,940
If you don't protect the bank, the river will take it away,

727
00:38:08,941 --> 00:38:11,940
and eventually the levee and whatever's behind the levee

728
00:38:11,941 --> 00:38:13,940
will fall into the river.

729
00:38:13,941 --> 00:38:16,940
Revetment is common in flood-prone areas,

730
00:38:16,941 --> 00:38:19,940
but this particular job is one of the most ambitious forms

731
00:38:19,941 --> 00:38:21,940
of revetment in the world.

732
00:38:21,941 --> 00:38:25,180
More than 3 million tons of concrete mat will be lowered

733
00:38:25,280 --> 00:38:26,680
into the river.

734
00:38:26,780 --> 00:38:29,980
This will keep powerful currents from eroding the bank.

735
00:38:30,080 --> 00:38:31,980
It will also hold the river in place.

736
00:38:34,680 --> 00:38:37,480
The mat is lowered over the edge of a barge,

737
00:38:37,580 --> 00:38:41,600
from the shoreline to the deepest part of the river,

738
00:38:41,700 --> 00:38:45,100
where it will settle on the bottom.

739
00:38:45,200 --> 00:38:49,700
In other words, the core is basically paving the Mississippi.

740
00:38:49,701 --> 00:38:52,540
This concrete mat has already been laid in the water,

741
00:38:52,640 --> 00:38:54,540
and it's protecting this section of the riverbank

742
00:38:54,640 --> 00:38:57,040
from being eroded by the river.

743
00:38:57,140 --> 00:38:59,980
Behind me, they're laying a new section

744
00:39:00,080 --> 00:39:01,840
which will overlap this one.

745
00:39:01,940 --> 00:39:03,540
We overlap each section like shingles

746
00:39:03,640 --> 00:39:05,720
on the roof of a house so that the river doesn't

747
00:39:05,820 --> 00:39:08,220
get an opportunity to get under it, flip it over.

748
00:39:11,080 --> 00:39:14,220
The job requires three huge barges.

749
00:39:14,320 --> 00:39:16,820
The mat barge is a delivery boat.

750
00:39:16,920 --> 00:39:19,700
It transports squares of concrete to the desert

751
00:39:19,701 --> 00:39:22,600
job site.

752
00:39:22,601 --> 00:39:32,380
The mooring barge simply serves as an anchor for the main workboat, or launching barge.

753
00:39:32,381 --> 00:39:39,540
This is where the action is, as concrete squares are assembled into a massive, flexible, 140-foot

754
00:39:39,541 --> 00:39:40,540
wide mat.

755
00:39:40,541 --> 00:39:45,540
The loaded mat barge has 585 squares on it.

756
00:39:45,541 --> 00:39:48,800
The four cranes over here will pick up two squares at a time,

757
00:39:49,280 --> 00:39:51,200
set them down to the deck one at a time,

758
00:39:51,560 --> 00:39:54,720
and then 36 gun runners use a pneumatic tying tool

759
00:39:54,721 --> 00:39:56,100
to tie the matches together.

760
00:39:56,760 --> 00:39:58,580
And about every four or five minutes,

761
00:39:58,840 --> 00:40:01,300
they'll launch 25 foot down all day long.

762
00:40:03,480 --> 00:40:07,380
The workhorse of this entire operation is this simple tool,

763
00:40:07,920 --> 00:40:10,420
aptly called a pneumatic tying tool.

764
00:40:10,940 --> 00:40:13,760
When a worker inserts a piece of wire and pushes a lever,

765
00:40:13,761 --> 00:40:17,960
An air motor engages a set of gears that twist steel wire

766
00:40:18,060 --> 00:40:20,560
around an anchor.

767
00:40:20,660 --> 00:40:25,100
These are the gears that are actually inside,

768
00:40:25,200 --> 00:40:28,900
rotating opposite directions to wrap that wire around and

769
00:40:29,000 --> 00:40:33,420
around the cable and the two mat wires.

770
00:40:33,520 --> 00:40:36,120
The Corps of Engineers has been placing revetment

771
00:40:36,220 --> 00:40:39,320
in the Mississippi River since the late 1800s.

772
00:40:39,420 --> 00:40:42,120
But back then, the crew didn't have concrete mat at its

773
00:40:42,121 --> 00:40:45,320
at its disposal. The earliest mat was actually

774
00:40:45,321 --> 00:40:49,320
made of willow trees that they would weave together and sink

775
00:40:49,321 --> 00:40:52,160
with rocks. Workers tied willow

776
00:40:52,161 --> 00:40:55,960
branches together to form flexible wooden mats that covered several

777
00:40:55,961 --> 00:40:58,760
acres. The enormous mats were then

778
00:40:58,761 --> 00:41:03,680
floated into position and sunk, using rocks as weights.

779
00:41:03,681 --> 00:41:07,720
It was incredibly labor-intensive, but remarkably effective.

780
00:41:07,721 --> 00:41:11,440
In fact, the willow mat still exists at the river's bottom.

781
00:41:12,780 --> 00:41:18,360
The part of the willow mat that stays wet all the time isn't exposed to the oxygen, so it doesn't rot.

782
00:41:18,960 --> 00:41:21,280
And so it'll last a very long time underwater.

783
00:41:22,400 --> 00:41:27,300
Today, the core is basically covering the old wooden mat with modern concrete mats.

784
00:41:29,680 --> 00:41:32,820
The goal is to pave the entire Lower Mississippi River.

785
00:41:37,720 --> 00:41:43,320
But the job doesn't end there.

786
00:41:43,321 --> 00:41:50,840
The Corps will constantly conduct surveys of the river's bottom and replace mats as needed.

787
00:41:50,841 --> 00:41:58,220
This massive open-ended project safeguards infrastructure along the river, and it also

788
00:41:58,221 --> 00:42:06,120
protects the Atchafalaya, one of our last great swamps.

789
00:42:06,121 --> 00:42:08,520
And that's what Swamp Tech is all about.

790
00:42:08,521 --> 00:42:12,260
It's man's struggle to live with, protect, and sometimes

791
00:42:12,261 --> 00:42:15,200
tame Mother Nature.

792
00:42:15,201 --> 00:42:18,800
It's an ambitious endeavor.

793
00:42:18,801 --> 00:42:21,960
But the payoff is spectacular.

